%% ============================================================
%% 22.1 Introduction
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:gradual-intro}

The choice between static and dynamic typing has traditionally been seen as
a binary decision: a language is either statically typed (like Haskell or
Java) or dynamically typed (like Python or Ruby).  But this dichotomy is
false.  \emph{Gradual typing} provides a spectrum between static and
dynamic, allowing programmers to choose the appropriate level of type
checking for each part of their program.

Gradual typing was introduced by Siek and Taha~\cite{siek2006gradual} and
has since been adopted by TypeScript, Python (via type hints and mypy),
Hack (Facebook's PHP variant), and many other languages.  It represents one
of the most significant developments in programming language design in the
twenty-first century.

In this chapter, we develop the theory and practice of gradual typing.
We begin with the motivation: why combine static and dynamic typing?  We
then develop the formal framework: the gradual guarantee, type casts, blame
tracking, and sound gradual typing.  We survey implementations: Typed
Racket, TypeScript, and Pyright.

\begin{definition}[Gradual Typing]\label{def:gradual-typing}
  \emph{Gradual typing} is a type system design that allows each program
  component to be either statically typed or dynamically typed, with
  well-defined interactions between the two.  The type $\texttt{?}$ (or
  \texttt{Any}, \texttt{dynamic}) represents an unknown or untyped value.
\end{definition}

%% ============================================================
%% 22.2 Motivation: Dynamic Meets Static
%% ============================================================
\section{Motivation: Dynamic Meets Static}\label{sec:gradual-motivation}

\subsection{The Limitations of Pure Approaches}\label{sec:limitations}

\textbf{Purely static typing} has limitations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Prototyping}: Type annotations slow down rapid exploration.
  \item \textbf{Legacy code}: Migrating a large untyped codebase to a
    typed language is expensive.
  \item \textbf{Heterogeneous data}: Processing data of unknown or varying
    types (JSON, configuration files) is awkward.
  \item \textbf{Metaprogramming}: Reflection, eval, and code generation
    resist static typing.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Purely dynamic typing} has limitations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Late error detection}: Type errors are found at runtime,
    possibly in production.
  \item \textbf{Documentation}: Without types, the programmer must read
    the code to understand what a function expects.
  \item \textbf{Optimization}: The compiler cannot use type information for
    optimization.
  \item \textbf{Refactoring}: Without types, large-scale changes are risky.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Gradual Solution}\label{sec:gradual-solution}

Gradual typing offers the best of both worlds:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Static where it matters}: Critical code paths are
    statically typed for safety and performance.
  \item \textbf{Dynamic where needed}: Prototypes, legacy code, and
    metaprogramming can remain dynamically typed.
  \item \textbf{Migration path}: Code can be gradually migrated from
    dynamic to static typing.
  \item \textbf{Interoperability}: Typed and untyped code can call each
    other.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Gradual Typing in Python]\label{ex:gradual-python}
  \begin{verbatim}
  # Untyped (dynamic)
  def process(data):
      return data["result"]
  
  # Gradually typed
  def process(data: dict[str, Any]) -> Any:
      return data["result"]
  
  # Fully typed
  def process(data: dict[str, Result]) -> Result:
      return data["result"]
  \end{verbatim}
  The programmer can choose the level of typing for each function.
\end{example}

%% ============================================================
%% 22.3 The Gradual Guarantee
%% ============================================================
\section{The Gradual Guarantee}\label{sec:gradual-guarantee}

\subsection{The Consistency Relation}\label{sec:consistency}

\begin{definition}[Consistency]\label{def:consistency}
  Two types $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$ are \emph{consistent} (written $\tau_1
  \sim \tau_2$) if they could plausibly be the same type, given that
  $\texttt{?}$ represents an unknown type:
  \begin{align*}
    \texttt{?} & \sim \tau \quad \text{for all } \tau \\
    \tau & \sim \texttt{?} \quad \text{for all } \tau \\
    \tau & \sim \tau \quad \text{for all } \tau \\
    \tau_1 \to \tau_2 & \sim \sigma_1 \to \sigma_2
      \quad \text{if } \tau_1 \sim \sigma_1 \text{ and } \tau_2 \sim \sigma_2
  \end{align*}
\end{definition}

Consistency is reflexive and symmetric but \emph{not transitive}:
$\Int \sim \texttt{?}$ and $\texttt{?} \sim \Bool$, but $\Int \not\sim \Bool$.

\subsection{The Gradual Guarantee}\label{sec:gradual-guarantee-detail}

\begin{definition}[Gradual Guarantee]\label{def:gradual-guarantee}
  A gradually typed language satisfies the \emph{gradual guarantee} if:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dynamic gradual guarantee}: Removing type annotations
      from a program does not introduce new static errors.  The program
      behaves as a dynamically typed program.
    \item \textbf{Static gradual guarantee}: Adding type annotations to a
      program does not change its dynamic behavior (except to catch errors
      earlier).
  \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Gradual Guarantee (Siek and Taha)]\label{thm:gradual-guarantee}
  Let $p$ be a program and $p'$ be the same program with some type
  annotations removed (replaced by $\texttt{?}$).  Then:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item If $p$ is statically well-typed, then $p'$ is also well-typed.
    \item If $p$ evaluates to a value $v$, then $p'$ evaluates to the same
      value $v$ (modulo casts).
  \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{insight}
The gradual guarantee ensures that adding type annotations is always safe:
it can only catch more errors, never change the program's behavior.  This
is what makes gradual typing practical: programmers can add types
incrementally, with confidence that existing code will continue to work.
\end{insight}

%% ============================================================
%% 22.4 Type Casts and Blame Tracking
%% ============================================================
\section{Type Casts and Blame Tracking}\label{sec:casts-blame}

\subsection{The Need for Casts}\label{sec:need-for-casts}

When typed and untyped code interact, the boundary between them must be
enforced.  This is done through \emph{casts}: runtime type checks that
verify values crossing the boundary.

\begin{definition}[Cast]\label{def:cast}
  A \emph{cast} $\langle \tau_1 \Rightarrow \tau_2 \rangle^l$ is a runtime
  operation that checks whether a value of type $\tau_1$ is also of type
  $\tau_2$.  The label $l$ identifies the source location for error
  reporting.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Cast at the Boundary]\label{ex:cast-boundary}
  \begin{verbatim}
  # Typed function
  def add(x: int, y: int) -> int:
      return x + y
  
  # Untyped caller
  result = add("hello", "world")  # Cast error at boundary
  \end{verbatim}
  At the call boundary, the arguments are cast from $\texttt{?}$ to
  $\Int$.  The cast fails because \texttt{"hello"} is not an integer.
\end{example}

\subsection{Blame Tracking}\label{sec:blame-tracking}

\begin{definition}[Blame]\label{def:blame}
  \emph{Blame tracking} is the mechanism for determining which side of a
  typed/untyped boundary is responsible for a cast failure.  The blame is
  assigned to the side that introduced the inconsistent type.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Blame Assignment]\label{ex:blame}
  \begin{verbatim}
  # typed_module.py
  def process(x: int) -> int:
      return x + 1
  
  # untyped_module.py
  from typed_module import process
  result = process("hello")  # BLAME: untyped_module.py
  \end{verbatim}
  The blame is assigned to \texttt{untyped\_module.py} because it passed a
  string where an integer was expected.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[Blame Correctness]\label{thm:blame-correctness}
  If a cast fails, the blame is always assigned to the correct party:
  the code that introduced the inconsistent type.  Typed code is never
  blamed for a cast failure.
\end{theorem}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Assigning Blame on Opposite Sides of a Function Cast}

A function value cannot be checked by running it on all possible
inputs at the boundary. Instead a wrapper checks arguments and
results when calls occur. Consider a dynamic provider $P$ that
exports a value to a client $C$ expecting $\Int\to\Int$.
The runtime has distinct integer and callable tags. An integer
crossing the boundary must carry the integer tag; the wrapper
uses the original tagged value when calling the provider. Model a
single positional argument, and consider only the checks at this
boundary: provider divergence and exceptions propagate as usual,
without being reclassified as type failures. The argument check
also guards calls from an untrusted caller in this model.

\begin{sudocode}[Wrap a dynamic integer function]
\Function{Wrap}{$v,P,C$}
  \If{$v$ is not callable}
    \State \Return blame $P$
  \EndIf
  \State \Return $\lambda a.\,\Call{Cross}{v,a,P,C}$
\EndFunction
\Function{Cross}{$v,a,P,C$}
  \If{$a$ lacks the integer tag}
    \State \Return blame $C$ \Comment{Argument flows to provider}
  \EndIf
  \State $r\gets v(a)$
  \If{$r$ lacks the integer tag}
    \State \Return blame $P$ \Comment{Result flows to client}
  \EndIf
  \State \Return $r$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
Suppose the provider returns the integer $6$ on input $2$,
but returns the string \texttt{zero} on input $0$. Wrapping
succeeds because the value is callable. On $2$ the wrapper
checks the input, calls the provider, and passes its tagged $6$
to the client. On $0$ the argument still passes, but the return
tag fails, blaming $P$. If the caller supplies the string
\texttt{two}, it is rejected with blame $C$ before the provider
runs. This last trace exercises a caller bypassing the static
discipline; a client verified to supply integers cannot take that
path through this wrapper.

\Cref{fig:visual-22} places a failing argument and a failing
result on opposite sides of the same function boundary.

