\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
A provider called through a wrapper receives only tagged integers,
and a normal result from the wrapper is also a tagged integer.
This explains the reversal of responsibility at the domain.
One successful call does not certify the function for future
arguments. This example uses flat tags and omits recursive wrappers
for function-valued arguments, coercion composition, and the
global hypotheses of a blame theorem.

\subsection{The Gradually Typed Lambda Calculus (GTLC)}\label{sec:gtlc}

The GTLC is the foundational calculus for gradual typing:

\begin{grammar}{GTLC}
  \production{e}{x \alt \lambda x:\tau.\, e \alt e_1\ e_2 \alt \langle e : \tau_1 \Rightarrow \tau_2 \rangle^l \alt \mathbf{blame}\ l}
\end{grammar}

\begin{grammar}{GTLC Types}
  \production{\tau}{\Int \alt \Bool \alt \tau_1 \to \tau_2 \alt \texttt{?}}
\end{grammar}

The operational semantics includes cast insertion and blame:

\begin{semrules}{GTLC Cast Semantics}
  \semrule{
    v \text{ has type } \tau_2
  }{
    \langle v : \tau_1 \Rightarrow \tau_2 \rangle^l \stepsto v
  }{\text{(Cast-OK)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    v \text{ does not have type } \tau_2
  }{
    \langle v : \tau_1 \Rightarrow \tau_2 \rangle^l \stepsto \mathbf{blame}\ l
  }{\text{(Cast-Fail)}}
\end{semrules}

%% ============================================================
%% 22.5 Gradual Type Inference
%% ============================================================
\section{Gradual Type Inference}\label{sec:gradual-inference}

\subsection{The Challenge}\label{sec:gradual-inference-challenge}

Type inference for gradually typed languages must handle the interaction
between static types and the dynamic type $\texttt{?}$.  The consistency
relation (which is not transitive) complicates unification.

\subsection{Approaches to Gradual Inference}\label{sec:gradual-inference-approaches}

\subsubsection{Optional Annotations}

The simplest approach: require type annotations at function boundaries,
infer types locally.  This is the approach taken by TypeScript and mypy.

\subsubsection{Constraint-Based Inference}

Generate constraints from the program and solve them:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Equality constraints}: $\tau_1 = \tau_2$ (as in HM).
  \item \textbf{Consistency constraints}: $\tau_1 \sim \tau_2$ (new for
    gradual typing).
  \item \textbf{Consistency does not determine a unique solution}: Multiple
    solutions may exist; the system must choose the most informative one.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{The Obstruction Problem}\label{sec:obstruction}

\begin{definition}[Obstruction]\label{def:obstruction}
  The \emph{obstruction problem} arises when consistency constraints
  prevent the inference of a more precise type.  For example, if
  $x : \texttt{?}$ and we use $x + 1$, the constraint $x \sim \Int$ does
  not determine $x : \Int$ (since $\texttt{?} \sim \Int$ is already
  satisfied).
\end{definition}

\subsection{Sound Gradual Typing}\label{sec:sound-gradual}

\begin{definition}[Sound Gradual Typing]\label{def:sound-gradual}
  A gradually typed language is \emph{sound} if well-typed programs never
  get stuck (in the sense of type errors).  Cast failures are caught at
  runtime and reported as blame.
\end{definition}

Sound gradual typing requires:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Cast insertion}: The compiler inserts casts at boundaries
    between typed and untyped code.
  \item \textbf{Blame tracking}: Cast failures are attributed to the
    correct party.
  \item \textbf{Type safety}: The cast insertion preserves type safety.
\end{itemize}

\begin{theorem}[Sound Gradual Typing (Siek et al.)]\label{thm:sound-gradual}
  The GTLC with cast insertion and blame tracking satisfies type soundness:
  if $\vdash e : \tau$ then $e$ either evaluates to a value, diverges, or
  raises blame, but never gets stuck.
\end{theorem}

%% ============================================================
%% 22.6 Implementations
%% ============================================================
\section{Implementations}\label{sec:gradual-implementations}

\subsection{Typed Racket}\label{sec:typed-racket}

\begin{historical}
Typed Racket~\cite{tobinhoch2008typed} was developed at Northwestern
University and PLT Inc.  It was the first language to combine gradual
typing with a sophisticated type system (including unions, intersections,
and occurrence typing).
\end{historical}

Typed Racket's key features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Occurrence typing}: The type of a variable is refined based
    on type tests:
    \begin{verbatim}
    (: f (-> (U String Number) String))
    (define (f x)
      (if (string? x)
          (string-upcase x)  ; x : String here
          (number->string x))) ; x : Number here
    \end{verbatim}
  \item \textbf{Interoperability}: Typed and untyped Racket modules can
    call each other; casts are inserted at boundaries.
  \item \textbf{Blame tracking}: Cast failures report the source of the
    inconsistent type.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{TypeScript}\label{sec:typescript}

\begin{historical}
TypeScript was developed by Microsoft, first released in 2012 by Anders
Hejlsberg~\cite{typescript2012}.  It adds a static type system to JavaScript
while maintaining full backward compatibility.
\end{historical}

TypeScript's type system features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Structural typing}: Types are compatible based on their
    structure, not their names.
  \item \textbf{Union and intersection types}: \texttt{string | number},
    \texttt{A \& B}.
  \item \textbf{Type narrowing}: Control flow analysis refines types.
  \item \textbf{Generics}: Parametric polymorphism with constraints.
  \item \texttt{any}: The dynamic type; disables type checking.
  \item \texttt{unknown}: A type-safe alternative to \texttt{any}; requires
    type narrowing before use.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[TypeScript Gradual Typing]\label{ex:typescript}
  \begin{verbatim}
  // Untyped JavaScript (valid TypeScript)
  function add(x, y) { return x + y; }
  
  // Gradually typed
  function add(x: any, y: any): any { return x + y; }
  
  // Fully typed
  function add(x: number, y: number): number { return x + y; }
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Pyright and mypy}\label{sec:pyright-mypy}

Python's gradual typing ecosystem includes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Type hints} (PEP 484): Standard syntax for type annotations.
  \item \textbf{mypy}: The original type checker for Python.
  \item \textbf{Pyright}: Microsoft's type checker (powers Pylance in VS
    Code).
  \item \textbf{Strict mode}: Increasing levels of type checking.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Python Gradual Typing]\label{ex:python-gradual}
  \begin{verbatim}
  # No types (dynamic)
  def process(data):
      return data["result"]
  
  # Partial types
  def process(data: dict) -> Any:
      return data["result"]
  
  # Full types
  def process(data: dict[str, Result]) -> Result:
      return data["result"]
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Comparison of Implementations}\label{sec:gradual-comparison}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Gradual Typing Implementations}
  \label{tab:gradual-comparison}
  \begin{tabular}{lllll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Language} & \textbf{Dynamic Type} & \textbf{Sound} & \textbf{Blame} & \textbf{Inference} \\
    \hline
    Typed Racket & \texttt{Any} & Yes & Yes & Local \\
    TypeScript & \texttt{any} & No & No & Structural \\
    Python/mypy & \texttt{Any} & Partial & Partial & Local \\
    Hack & \texttt{dynamic} & Partial & Partial & Local \\
    Gradualizer (Erlang) & \texttt{any()} & Yes & Yes & Local \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{The Soundness Gap}\label{sec:soundness-gap}

\begin{definition}[Soundness Gap]\label{def:soundness-gap}
  The \emph{soundness gap} is the difference between the type system's
  guarantees and the actual runtime behavior.  TypeScript has a significant
  soundness gap: the type system allows certain programs that will fail at
  runtime.
\end{definition}

TypeScript's soundness gaps include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{any} disables all checking.
  \item Structural typing allows unsound coercions.
  \item \texttt{null} and \texttt{undefined} are assignable to any type
    (unless \texttt{strictNullChecks} is enabled).
  \item Type assertions (\texttt{as}) bypass checking.
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
The soundness gap in TypeScript is a deliberate design choice: it allows
TypeScript to be adopted incrementally in existing JavaScript codebases.
A fully sound type system would require more annotations and reject more
programs, which would reduce adoption.  The trade-off between soundness and
practicality is a central tension in gradual typing.
\end{insight}

%% ============================================================
%% 22.7 Summary
%% ============================================================
\section{Summary}\label{sec:gradual-summary}

Gradual typing represents a paradigm shift in the static vs.\ dynamic
typing debate.  Rather than forcing a binary choice, it provides a spectrum
that allows programmers to choose the appropriate level of type checking
for each part of their program.

The theoretical foundation of gradual typing rests on three pillars:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{The consistency relation}: Defines when two types are
    compatible in the presence of the dynamic type.
  \item \textbf{The gradual guarantee}: Ensures that adding type
    annotations is always safe.
  \item \textbf{Blame tracking}: Ensures that type errors at boundaries
    are attributed to the correct party.
\end{enumerate}

Implementations of gradual typing range from fully sound (Typed Racket) to
pragmatically unsound (TypeScript).  Each makes different trade-offs
between soundness, usability, and adoption.

Gradual typing is likely to become the dominant paradigm for type systems
in the coming decades.  As the software industry recognizes that the
static/dynamic dichotomy is false, languages will increasingly provide
gradual typing as a way to get the benefits of both worlds.
